## Standing overrides for this unattended run

- Skip any session-start ritual the repository's instructions ask for (ticket fetches, read-throughs, persona
  announcements). Do NOT
  checkout or pull {{BASE}} or create branches: the worktree is set up. Never touch other worktrees.
- Everything else in the repository's own instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, CONTRIBUTING, its docs) applies: its
  architecture and patterns, its test conventions, its build checks.
- Scope is exactly `.fixloop/brief.md`. Don't fix neighbouring issues; record them with `task.py note` ("Out of scope, seen: ...").
- A brief with a "The design (binding)" section was planned with EGM: the design doc it names is the spec, approved by
  {{OWNER}}, and other chains are building the rest of it right now from the same doc. Change only the files the brief
  lists (new tests for them are fine); a change any other file needs is `task.py needs "<file> and why"`, never an edit.
  red writes a failing test for each task and journey the brief lists, not only for defects. Where the brief and the
  design disagree, or the design doesn't settle something, `task.py needs`.
- No user-visible design change beyond what the brief allows (reusing existing, signed-off labels and states is fine).
  No persisted-data change (new, renamed or removed stored fields, collections or indexes) unless the brief says so.
  If the fix turns out to need either: `task.py needs "<what and why>"` and stop.
- No subagents. To ask {{OWNER}} something, `task.py ask "<Title>" "<question>" "<recommended answer>" "<other answer>" ...`:
  it shows on this branch's live page, you carry on with the recommended answer, and the pr phase waits until it is
  answered. Ask that way only when the answer can't change what users see or what is stored, and redoing that part is
  cheap. Anything else (a visible change, a stored field, the brief's scope) is `task.py needs "<exact ask>"`, which
  stops the chain.
- Start every session with `task.py show`. An `OWNER ANSWER` note comes first: if it isn't what you carried on with, redo
  that part (test first) before anything else.
- {{OWNER}} follows this branch on its live page, which shows your phase lines, notes and questions as you write them.
  Write them in plain words: what changed for the person using the app or for the system, and where in the process. Keep
  file:line and method names for commits and HANDOFF.md. A question's title is short, with Every Word Capitalized; its
  text says what happened, with real ids, and where it bites.
- The brief's Tasks say what done means. Tick each with `task.py done T1 "<how it was proved>"` once its test passes; every
  task is ticked before the pr phase.
- Never apply a review comment on your own. Record each with a proposed resolution (`task.py review`); {{OWNER}} decides.
- You can't publish artifacts or file tickets: say what should be filed in a `task.py note` and in the PR body.

Run task.py and gate.py with these exact paths (they are allow-listed): `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/task.py ...`, `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/gate.py ...`.

## The phases (record each with `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/task.py phase <name> "<what>"`)

1. **red**: read the brief, the repository's testing guidance and its docs for the area. Write failing tests that
   reproduce each defect in the brief. Run only those, with the project's test runner and its filter for one test or
   file (the gate's commands in `{{RUN}}/project.json` show how the project runs its tests), and confirm they fail for
   the right reason. Commit them: `test(<area>): <defect> (red)`.
2. **green**: fix the code; the filtered tests pass; build what you touched (the gate builds and tests everything).
   Find every caller and every test of any signature you change (constructor, function, interface, schema) and update
   them in the same change. Commit: `fix(<area>): <defect summary>`.
3. **docs**: update the matching docs sections (and the decisions log if the brief records a decision). Commit.
4. **gate**: `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/gate.py --start`, then poll `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/gate.py --status` with
   `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/task.py wait 300` between polls (it takes a while; other branches share the test slots).
   If the verdict line says a check was "not checked on this machine" ({{OWNER}}'s decision, loop.conf GATE_SKIP), that is
   a pass for this phase, never yours to change: the pr and ci phases below say what it costs.
   Unexpected failures: fix them if your change caused them; if they also fail on {{BASE}}, note it and carry on.
   A "known flaky" test must pass when run alone: run it filtered and say so. A test the gate lists as failing "on this
   platform every time" is not re-run: it can't pass on this machine, and CI runs it. A build that is incomplete, a filtered grep
   that "shows nothing" or a suite that ran zero tests is not a pass: read the gate's verdict.
5. **security**: run the `security-review` skill over the branch diff; fix high-confidence findings in scope, note the rest.
6. **pr**: if `task.py phase pr` is refused because a question is still open, finish with
   `task.py needs "holding the pull request until {{OWNER}} answers Q<n>"`: nothing is published on an assumption.
   `git fetch origin` and `git merge origin/{{BASE}}` (resolve conflicts, then re-run the gate if anything merged);
   `git push -u origin <branch>`; `gh pr create --base {{BASE}} --title "<conventional title, no tool names>" --body-file .fixloop/pr-body.md`.
   The body stands on its own: what was wrong (defect ids, the tests that showed it), what changed,
   tests added, docs touched, what to re-verify after merge, and links to the plan and results pages as extra context
   only. If the gate left a step out (GATE_SKIP), the body says so in a "Not checked locally" line naming the step and
   what checks it instead (the gate's status prints the step's note). End it with
   `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`. Commit messages end with
   `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>`. If the repository's instructions ask for
   anything alongside a PR (a changelog entry, a tracking file), add it, commit and push. `task.py pr <N> <url>`.
7. **ci**: poll `gh pr checks <N>` (with `task.py wait 300`) until checks settle or ~45 minutes pass. A check that fails in
   2 to 3 s with zero steps is a GitHub Actions budget block, not your code: note it. A review bot that "passes" in seconds
   on a later push reviewed nothing new: note it. Then read every comment with `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/pr_read.py <N> comments`,
   `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/pr_read.py <N> reviews` and `{{PYTHON}} {{RUN}}/pr_read.py <N> issue` (read-only; `gh api` is not allowed).
   For each comment needing action: `task.py review "<who> #<comment id>: <what>, <file:line>" "<proposed resolution>"`.
   Always include the comment's numeric id: the main session matches your proposals to threads by it. Don't change the
   branch for them. If the gate skipped a step, the CI check that runs it instead must have passed before you finish
   (still pending: keep polling; failed: fix it as a gate failure). Finish with
   `task.py ready "<one line: PR, CI state, N review items>"`.
   Never install anything (brew, pip, sudo are denied): a missing tool is `task.py needs "<what and why>"`.

## When the owner has approved review items (a later chain on the same branch)

If `task.py show` has a note starting `APPROVED REVIEW ITEMS`, the PR already exists. Apply exactly the items it lists, as
their recorded proposals describe (or as the note amends them), nothing else: test-first where behaviour changes (phases
red and green again), `git merge origin/{{BASE}}` if it moved, run the gate, push to the same branch (no new PR), then poll
CI as in phase 7, record any NEW review comments as proposals, and finish with
`task.py ready "<pushed: commit ids per item; CI state; N new review items>"`. Don't reply to or resolve PR threads:
the main session does that, citing your commits.

## Design-only chains (the brief says `Mode: design`)

The brief asks for a mockup and/or a schema-change page, not code. Nothing is built until {{OWNER}} signs it off; a later
chain on this branch builds it. No code changes, no gate, no PR.

1. **design** (`task.py phase design "..."`): read what the brief names. For a mockup: the repository's design system
   or UI guidance if it has one, the signed-off mockups the brief points to (rows in `{{MOCKUPS_DIR}}/README.md`) and the
   code that renders the area today. For a schema page: the data-model docs and the code that defines the stored shapes
   the brief names, and `{{SCHEMA_DIR}}/README.md`.
   - **Mockup** → `{{MOCKUPS_DIR}}/<area>/<branch slug>.html`, a DELTA mockup: for each change, today (as the app renders
     it) beside the proposal, built from the signed-off designs and the existing components and copy, never redrawn from
     scratch. Number each choice (M1, M2, ...) with options and a recommendation, so {{OWNER}} can answer by number. Open
     questions at the top. Short: copy, states and where each appears. Every code reference is an absolute link to a
     commit: `https://github.com/{{GH_REPO}}/blob/<sha>/<path>#L<n>`. The page is published as a Claude Artifact, so: no
     `<!doctype>`, `<html>`, `<head>` or `<body>` tags (the host wraps the page; start with `<title>` then `<style>`); a
     `<title>` of 2 to 4 words naming the page; page-chrome colours as tokens on `:root`, redefined under
     `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` guarded by `:root:not([data-theme="light"])` and again under
     `:root[data-theme="dark"]` (the mocked app keeps its own theme colours); an explicit `body` background; fonts only
     from Google Fonts, no other external files; a side gutter of at least 16px at every width; readable at phone width
     with no horizontal page scroll (wide crops scroll inside their own box). Add a row to `{{MOCKUPS_DIR}}/README.md`:
     `**PROPOSED: awaiting sign-off ({{OWNER}})**`, a one-paragraph summary and the feature doc link.
   - **Schema page** → `{{SCHEMA_DIR}}/<branch slug>.md`: the model and the table or collection, each field added,
     removed, renamed or retyped (type, default, nullability), indexes and constraints, any tenant or ownership scoping,
     every read and write that changes, the migration or backfill (idempotent, safe to re-run, verified by an independent
     read), the deploy order, rollback, and what is NOT changed. Numbered choices (S1, S2, ...) with a recommendation where
     there is more than one sound shape. Add a row to `{{SCHEMA_DIR}}/README.md` with status `Proposed`.
   - Fields that are never stored (request and response shapes, projections) need no schema page.
2. Commit (`docs(<area>): <slug> mockup / schema page for sign-off`), push the branch, then
   `task.py ready "design: <paths>; choices M1..Mn / S1..Sn (recommendations); open questions: <list or none>"`.
   The main session publishes the page as an Artifact and collects the sign-off.

## When the owner has signed off the design (a later chain on a design branch)

If `task.py show` has a note starting `SIGNED OFF`, the mockup and/or schema page already on this branch is approved with
the choices that note lists. That note overrides the brief's "Mode: design" and "No code": this chain BUILDS it.

- **The spec is the signed-off page(s)** on this branch with the recorded choices, plus the brief's scope. Build what they
  say, nothing more. Copy, states and layout come from the page word for word. A visible choice the page doesn't settle,
  or a stored field it doesn't list, is not yours to make: `task.py needs "<exact ask>"` and stop. Questions the note
  sends elsewhere ("its own ticket", "the main session") stay out of scope.
- **UI:** follow the repository's design system or UI guidance; reuse existing components and tokens; every new state
  gets a component test (with the project's component-test tooling) and a stable test hook (e.g. a `data-testid`).
- **Schema:** implement exactly the page's fields, defaults and indexes; update the matching data-model docs in the docs
  phase. A migration or backfill the page specifies is written (and unit-tested when the page says so) but never run
  against any database; the PR lists the run order around the deploy.
- Then phases 1 to 7 as usual. In the pr phase, change this branch's README row(s) from "Building on" to
  "Built in [PR #N](url)". {{BASE}} may have moved since the design was drawn: `git merge origin/{{BASE}}` before red,
  and again before pr.

## Context guard and hand-off

Your context is metered after every tool call. At {{SOFT_PCT}}% you get `[context guard]` messages: finish only the step in
flight, record it, write `.fixloop/HANDOFF.md`, end your reply. At {{HARD_PCT}}% the session is ended for you; keep
`task.py` notes and commits current so nothing is lost. Keep dumps small: grep, filter test runs, never cat whole large
files or logs.

`.fixloop/HANDOFF.md` (≤ 40 lines): current phase and exact next step; files touched and why; tests added and their state;
anything the next session needs (a build quirk, a test filter that works, a design choice made inside the scope).
